CONTEXT FROM TEXTBOOK:
This figure pairs a flat structural drawing with a three-dimensional model of the same molecule, both cut by a horizontal plane. On the left, two carbons lettered "C" sit one above the other, joined by a vertical bond. The upper carbon bears an "H" above it on a dashed bond, an "HO" group at the left in magenta on a bold wedge, and a "CO2H" group at the right in green. The lower carbon repeats that arrangement flipped vertically: "HO" again at the left and "CO2H" at the right, but its "H" now points downward on a dashed bond. A pale blue parallelogram drawn edge-on slices horizontally between the two carbons, labeled "Symmetry plane". On the right the molecule appears as a ball-and-stick model, two dark gray carbons stacked vertically, each carrying a small blue sphere, a red oxygen with a second red sphere beyond it, and a branching green group, with an identical pale blue plane passing between them. Running the plane through both depictions asks the reader to check one half against the other. Because the halves are reflections, the molecule proves superimposable on its own mirror image despite having two chirality centers, the situation that defines a meso compound.

PROJECTION TYPE: axonometric
Use ordinary orthographic projection: setCameraView({ projection: 'orthographic', axisScreenAngles, heightFraction }). If the plan gives axis_screen_angles: null (a flat 2D plot/diagram), pass axisScreenAngles: null for a front-on view. Otherwise the plan does not measure per-figure angles — use a standard isometric pose: axisScreenAngles: { x: 30, z: 30 }. The source image is a PROJECTION of a 3D object: rebuild the true object — a rectangle stays a rectangle in world space — and let the camera do the projecting. Never bake the drawn parallelogram into the geometry, and never shear geometry. Omit zoom and call setCameraView after all geometry is added — the scaffold auto-fits from the projected bounding box.

ELEMENTS TO RECREATE IN 3D:
  - Left panel (2D structure): upper carbon lettered 'C' and lower carbon lettered 'C', stacked vertically
  - Vertical plain bond joining the two 'C' letters
  - Upper carbon: dashed bond pointing up to a blue 'H' label
  - Upper carbon: bold wedge pointing left to magenta 'HO' label
  - Upper carbon: plain bond pointing right to green 'CO2H' label
  - Lower carbon: dashed bond pointing down to a blue 'H' label
  - Lower carbon: bold wedge pointing left to magenta 'HO' label
  - Lower carbon: plain bond pointing right to green 'CO2H' label
  - Pale blue parallelogram drawn edge-on, slicing horizontally between the two carbons of the 2D structure
  - Black text label 'Symmetry plane' to the right of the 2D plane
  - Right panel (ball-and-stick model): upper dark-gray carbon sphere and lower dark-gray carbon sphere, stacked vertically and bonded by a light-gray cylinder
  - Upper carbon: small pale-blue hydrogen sphere on a short bond (pointing down-right toward viewer)
  - Upper carbon: red oxygen sphere up-left with a second smaller red sphere (hydroxyl H) beyond it
  - Upper carbon: green carboxyl carbon sphere up-right, branching to two further green oxygen spheres and a small green hydrogen sphere
  - Lower carbon: small pale-blue hydrogen sphere (pointing up-right toward viewer, mirrored)
  - Lower carbon: red oxygen sphere down-left with a second smaller red sphere beyond it
  - Lower carbon: green carboxyl group branching down-right, mirroring the upper one
  - Light-gray cylindrical bonds between all spheres
  - Pale blue transparent plane passing horizontally between the two model carbons, perpendicular to the central C–C bond

GEOMETRY NOTES (world-space scale, color palette, and any formula/value needed for correctness — build to these so sizes, zoom, and colors match the source; do not guess dimensions the notes already specify):
Meso tartaric acid, HO2C–CH(OH)–CH(OH)–CO2H. World scale: central C–C bond 1.6 units, drawn vertically along +Y with the upper carbon at (0, 0.8, 0) and lower at (0, -0.8, 0). Symmetry plane is a horizontal parallelogram in the y=0 plane, ~5 units wide (X) × 2 units deep (Z), pale blue #b9dff5 at ~45% opacity, edges slightly darker; it is perpendicular to the C–C bond and extends beyond the molecule on both sides. Upper carbon substituents (bond length 1.1): OH oxygen up-left at (-0.9, 1.4, 0.15) with its hydroxyl H beyond at (-1.5, 1.05, 0.3); H (small, 0.45× radius) down-right toward viewer at (0.55, 0.45, 0.85); carboxyl carbon up-right at (0.95, 1.4, -0.1), branching to two oxygens at (1.9, 1.15, 0.1) and (0.95, 2.4, -0.3) plus a small acid H. Lower carbon substituents are the exact mirror images through y=0 (negate Y of every upper-half position). Colors: carbon dark gray #4b5058; stereocenter hydrogens pale blue #7cc0e8; hydroxyl oxygen and its hydrogen red/dark red #b23225 and #8f231a; entire CO2H branch rendered green #3fbf5c (matching the green CO2H label in the 2D panel); bonds light gray #c9ced4 cylinders of radius 0.09, with a glossy specular highlight on each sphere. Sphere radii: C 0.38, O 0.34, green carboxyl atoms 0.34, H 0.20. 2D panel (shown in the same scene, offset to screen-left by ~4.5 units, drawn flat facing the camera in the XY plane): two black 'C' letters at (-4.5, 0.55) and (-4.5, -0.55); vertical plain bond between them; upper C has a short dashed vertical bond up to blue 'H' (#1f8fc2) at (-4.5, 1.25), a bold solid wedge left to magenta 'HO' (#d4197f) at (-5.5, 0.9), a plain bond right to green 'CO2H' (#127a4a) at (-3.4, 0.9); lower C mirrors this with the dashed bond and 'H' pointing down. A pale blue edge-on parallelogram at y=0 spans x from -5.6 to -3.6, with black text 'Symmetry plane' to its right at (-2.6, 0). Highlight color for clicked/partner groups: warm yellow glow #ffd24d outline.

DISCRETE CONTROLS (implement every one of these in #ui, each must work independently):
[
  {
    "id": "viewMode",
    "type": "toggle",
    "teaches": "The same internal mirror plane can be found in the flat line drawing and in the real 3D geometry — the two depictions describe one molecule, not two arguments.",
    "label": "Representation",
    "options": [
      "Structure only",
      "Model only",
      "Overlay"
    ],
    "default": "Structure only"
  },
  {
    "id": "pickGroup",
    "type": "click",
    "teaches": "Every substituent on the top stereocenter has an exact mirror partner on the bottom one, so the two centers have opposite configurations and their handedness cancels.",
    "teaches_note": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "orbitModel",
    "type": "orbit",
    "teaches": "Rotating the ball-and-stick model shows the mirror relationship holds in three dimensions, not just in the drawing's projection."
  },
  {
    "id": "reflectHalf",
    "type": "animation",
    "teaches": "Reflecting the top half through the plane lands it exactly on the bottom half — the molecule is superimposable on its mirror image, i.e. meso and achiral despite two chirality centers.",
    "label": "Reflect top half through the plane",
    "loop": false
  }
]

DEMO STEPS (tween through these using goToStep(); each step drives the controls above):
[
  {
    "title": "The flat drawing",
    "narration": "Two stereocenters stacked vertically: each carries HO on a wedge to the left and CO2H to the right, but the H's point in opposite directions. A pale blue plane slices between them.",
    "state": {
      "viewMode": "Structure only"
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Same molecule in 3D",
    "narration": "The ball-and-stick model shows the same arrangement: gray carbons, a small blue H, a red OH, and a green CO2H on each center, cut by the same horizontal plane.",
    "state": {
      "viewMode": "Model only"
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Check group against group",
    "narration": "Click HO, H, or CO2H on the upper center; its mirror partner below lights up. Every substituent is matched across the plane.",
    "state": {
      "viewMode": "Overlay"
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Fold it over",
    "narration": "Reflecting the top half through the plane superimposes it on the bottom half — the internal mirror makes this meso compound achiral.",
    "state": {
      "viewMode": "Model only"
    }
  }
]

CAMERA VIEW (framing only). Call setCameraView({ projection, ...these }) AFTER all geometry is added. Keep the projection MECHANISM from PROJECTION TYPE above (orthographic / sheared-oblique / perspective). For perspective these params set the pose (azimuth/elevation/roll); for the two parallel projections pose is a standard default (see PROJECTION TYPE above), and these params set only roll and framing (heightFraction):
{
  "heightFraction": 0.65
}

NOTES: Build the 3D model from THREE.SphereGeometry + CylinderGeometry grouped per substituent so a whole group (OH, H, CO2H) can be highlighted as a unit; give each group a userData tag {half:'top'|'bottom', group:'OH'|'H'|'CO2H'} so a click finds its mirror partner by matching group and flipping half. Plane: PlaneGeometry rotated -PI/2 about X, MeshBasicMaterial transparent, depthWrite false, side DoubleSide, rendered after the molecule to avoid z-fighting. 2D panel drawn as CanvasTexture sprites/planes (or HTML overlay aligned to the canvas) so the colored labels and bold-wedge/dashed-bond typography match the source; wedge as a filled triangle, dashed bond as a stack of short line segments. Overlay mode fades the 2D panel to ~35% opacity behind/beside the model rather than removing it. Reflect animation: lerp a clone of the top-half group through a scale(1,-1,1) mirror matrix over ~1.5 s, ghosting it in translucent cyan so the learner sees it settle exactly onto the bottom half; add a brief 'superimposable' flash on completion. Orbit limited to ±60° elevation so the plane never becomes exactly edge-on-invisible; soft directional key light + ambient fill for the glossy ball-and-stick look.

